---
title: "Mobility budget mandatory 2027: prepare TCO and your rules"
date: 2026-11-23
lang: en
category: Payroll & HR
excerpt: "From January 2027, SMEs with 50+ employees must offer a mobility budget. TCO, pillars, communication: what you need to implement now."
published: true
author: AgiFid
---

The mobility budget becomes mandatory on 1 January 2027 for companies with 50 or more employees.
Companies with 15 to 49 employees have until 1 January 2028,
whilst those with fewer than 15 employees are not concerned.

The timeline appears clear. But affected companies often underestimate the time needed for implementation: calculating the TCO by vehicle category, formalising it in employment regulations, individual communication to eligible employees.
A transition period is provided until 31 December 2027 for companies with fewer than 50 employees.
Little cause for reassurance:
implementing a mobility budget always requires a certain amount of time.

## Who is concerned?

The obligation only applies to employers who have already made company cars available for at least 36 consecutive months.
If you have never offered a company car or have done so for less than 36 months, you are not concerned,
regardless of your company's size.

The obligation only concerns employers who provide, or have provided, for at least 36 months at least one company car to at least one employee.
Cars obtained through salary exchange are not eligible. Your employees retain the right to refuse the option:
employees are never obliged to actually use it.

## Calculate the TCO, from now on

The mobility budget is based on the Total Cost of Ownership (TCO) of the company car, including leasing, maintenance, insurance, fuel and all other costs related to the vehicle.
The authorities set a relative ceiling of maximum 20% of gross salary and an absolute ceiling of €17,245, with a lower limit of €3,233.

The TCO can be calculated on an actual or flat-rate basis.
Since 1 January 2024, the employer chooses between a calculation on actual costs and a flat-rate calculation, and this choice applies to all employees for three years.
It's better to anticipate: without figures by vehicle category, you will not be able to define the individual amount of each mobility budget.

## The three spending pillars

The Belgian mobility budget rests on 3 pillars: ecological vehicle, sustainable means of transport and balance in cash.

**Pillar 1:**
Since 1 January 2026, only 100% electric vehicles are eligible for Pillar 1.

**Pillar 2:**
The budget can be used to finance trains, the metro, trams, buses, a bicycle, certain shared vehicles.
Since 2026, even shared cars must be electric.

**Pillar 3:**
If part of the budget has not been spent, the balance can be paid to the employee at the end of the year. It is then subject to a special contribution of 38.07%, but not to income tax.

## What must be documented before 1 January

You must formalise three elements in your internal policy or employment regulations: the TCO calculation method (actual or flat-rate), the open pillars (all or a selection), and the eligible job categories.

The employer must respond in writing to the request for a mobility budget submitted by an employee.
Prepare a communication template that recalls the individual amount, accessible pillars, and practical procedures.

The employer may defer the actual launch of the mobility budget for a specific employee until the end of the duration of the leasing contract or another contract for the company car made available.
This flexibility gives you time to align the rollout with your fleet deadlines.

## Links with Odoo

If your payroll management runs on Odoo, create a "Mobility budget" category in your Payroll module now. You will then be able to automate the tracking of expenses by pillar and the calculation of the Pillar 3 balance at year-end. Also provide a field to record each employee's eligibility start date: this will facilitate the management of requests.

---

**In practice:** the obligation comes into force on 1 January 2027, but the compliance deadline provides breathing space until 31 December 2027 for smaller structures. The fact remains that calculating the TCO, defining the policy and internal communication take several months. Best to start in December.
